% ============================================================================
% cell-division-genetic-outcome — for mitosis and meiosis, the genetic
% property of the daughter cells each process yields, as the SAME NHGRI
% sentences also state (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `cell-division-daughter-cells.adj`
% (`cell_division_daughter_cells(process, count)`, HOW MANY daughter cells
% each process yields — mitosis → 2, meiosis → 4). That table's own header
% deliberately quotes, verbatim, the OTHER clause of each defining sentence
% "for honesty" while explicitly stating it is NOT what the count-only
% schema extracts — a second fact sitting unused in plain sight:
%
%   mitosis → genetically_identical
%     "Mitosis is generally followed by equal division of the cell's content
%      into two daughter cells that HAVE IDENTICAL GENOMES."
%
%   meiosis → haploid
%     "During meiosis, each diploid cell undergoes two rounds of division to
%      yield four HAPLOID daughter cells — the gametes."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? cell_division_genetic_outcome(mitosis, $Outcome)   % genetically_identical
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `cell_division_genetic_outcome(process, outcome)` carrying the citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the outcome WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on any process that is not one of these two (it never invents an
% outcome — `binary_fission`, the prokaryotic process, is deliberately NOT a
% row here either, matching the parent table's own abstention boundary).
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an outcome and recall its process:
%
%     ? cell_division_genetic_outcome($Process, haploid)
%       % meiosis
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME two NIH National Human
% Genome Research Institute (NHGRI) "Genetics Glossary" sentences already
% quoted inside `cell-division-daughter-cells.adj`'s own header — no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the genetic-outcome half of
% each already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `cell-division-daughter-cells.adj` already carries (a primary U.S.
% government reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table cell_division_genetic_outcome {
    columns process, outcome

    row (mitosis, genetically_identical)   % NHGRI: "Mitosis is generally followed by equal division of the cell's content into two daughter cells that have identical genomes."
    row (meiosis, haploid)                 % NHGRI: "During meiosis, each diploid cell undergoes two rounds of division to yield four haploid daughter cells -- the gametes."

    source "Mitosis is generally followed by equal division of the cell's content into two daughter cells that have identical genomes."
    locator "https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Mitosis"
    trust authoritative

    cites "During meiosis, each diploid cell undergoes two rounds of division to yield four haploid daughter cells -- the gametes." locator "https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Meiosis"
}
